        "I kept a solid pace to stay warm and kept my breaks short. They ended up being more of a nuisance in the winter conditions more than a benefit. Whenever I stopped for more than a few minutes it became necessary to layer up as my back, usually covered by my backpack, became exposed to the cold, causing my sweat to chill me. There was that as well aiming for Blood Mountain, 17-miles away, as motivation to just keep moving.",
        "Along the way, I bumped into a pair of day-hikers. One man was an athletic older fellow. His friend was alarmingly obese for the pace they were hiking. The larger man explained to me that he had dropped 200 pounds since he started working off his weight and was working hard to achieve a weight of 200 pounds. I was pretty damn impressed. I can't imagine many people who end up over 400 pounds manage to drop their weight naturally. When they shared that there were two other thru-hikers about a mile ahead of me, I congratulated the man and rushed off to catch up.",
        "It wasn't long after Eve's departure that I found myself eager to find good hiking company. The first man I encountered I spoke to only briefly. He was from Germany and spoke very little English. Being a bit older and new to hiking, he was planning to make it as far as he could until September before returning home.",
