        "It's pretty cold out here. It seems silly to think of how much I would dread leaving the warmth of my bed for work on cold mornings at home. There I had the convenience of a shower and a warm room to dress in. The lack of modern luxuries was redeemed by having no job, schedule, or any responsibility other than to hike north, eat snacks, and not die.",
        "It takes about an hour each morning to put on my layers, pack, and eat a dry breakfast before hitting the trail. I'm often delayed by the need to warm my hands, usually by shoving them into my pants, to complete simple tasks like tying my shoes.",
        'Thankfully, my feet and the rest of my body return to comfortable temperatures after about twenty minutes of hiking. The six-inch-deep snow, sometimes higher in wind drifts, demands more energy as I hike, causing my body to warm quickly. Once I get going everything seems fine so long as I take breaks quickly to avoid getting cold again. Food helps a lot too, especially of the variety-packed with fat and sugar. How fortunate I am to have been born in the era of Snickers Bars, proclaimed to be "one of the great achievements of Western Civilization," by Exum Guide Jack Turner.',
        "In my determination to make it to North Carolina today I completed 15.5 miles with 2800 vertical feet of total elevation gain. The entire hike was through deep snow that brought with it an assortment of frustrations and physical repercussions. There were a remarkable number of fallen trees and debris scattered about the trail as well. Polar vortex events have a pretty strong influence on hiking stoke, among other things.",
        "Nights have also provided opportunities to practice patience. Mice. Everywhere. Not an evening has passed without the evasive little bastards rummaging through packs, running across my sleeping bag, and on one occasion, my face. I was often angry enough to leave my bed in a futile effort to destroy them. I have little doubt that the rodent problem derives from hikers keeping food in the shelters, something I have chosen not to do in the future. Lately, I've been pitching my bivy inside the shelter to keep the little creeps off me.",
        "Despite all the mayhem, it could certainly be worse. I'm not waking up for work or school or war. Winter has its unique charm, it's a novelty to be on the trail before the crowds. Nonetheless, I'm looking forward to warmer weather.",
