      slug: "gnar",
      legacySlug: "gnar",
      title: "Gnar",
      date: "2014-06-03",
      dateLabel: "June 3, 2014",
      paragraphs: [
        "As I write this, I am finishing the final portions of a quart and a half of ice cream. I have to say, the ability to consume whatever I desire in what would otherwise be irresponsible quantities has been one of my favorite qualities about thru-hiking. As I departed from Hanover, I made sure to grab a pint of Ben & Jerry's to eat on my way back into the mountains. I was feeling really good that day, having given my legs a chance to rest along shorter, 18-22 mile days on top of so much food, so it only made sense to double down and make it better.",
        "400 miles to go now. I'm at Hikers Welcome Hostel repairing my gear, punishing some food, and hanging out with the other hikers. Tomorrow I head into the White Mountains which some say is the hardest part of the AT. The day begins with a 7-mile climb up 4,000 feet before sending me down an even steeper grade for some more gnar that will continue over the next week at least. I'm stoked, nothing gets me amped like really big hikes. I've been eyeballing Mt. Moosilauke in the AWOL guide since the first week of my thru-hike. Although its summit is less than 5,000 vertical feet in elevation, the hike itself is easily as big as many of the 14,000-foot peaks I've hiked in Colorado.",
        "I am feeling most excited for all of the alpine summits that I'll traverse over the next few days (moving on to a bag of potato chips now). No longer will I needlessly climb thousands of feet just to arrive on a heavily forested, viewless peak. Instead, I will needlessly climb thousands of feet to emerge above treeline and gaze upon hundreds of miles of wilderness in every direction. Of course, this is all provided that the weather is good. The Whites get snow every month of the year and I'm going to remain mindful of the fact that Mount Washington once held the highest wind speed record in the world at 231 mph. It might get a little crazy, but crazy can be fun.",
        "I've gone 1,700-something miles without seeing a bear until two days ago when I saw five in a single day. I was making my way up a needless climb that morning when I heard a loud crashing to my left. I suspected a porcupine since I've been seeing a lot of those, but as I looked intently through the trees I saw maybe a hundred feet away from a bear the size of my pack running away from me up the hill with its cub in hot pursuit.",
        "After making my way to the top of that not so scenic climb and being swarmed by insects I once again heard a disturbance nearby. There it was, a big fat bear sitting behind a tree. We made intense eye contact as his two buddies booked it up the hill away from me. As I proceeded to take his picture he began to snort at me in frustration for ruining his happy bear fun time. I was going to challenge him to fisticuffs but continued onward as there were miles to crush and no time to waste.",
        "I've been hiking with Caribou since Hanover which has truly been a pleasure. His stories of Alaskan adventures, Denali, Aconcagua, and hiking the highest peaks in each of the 50 states kept me amused day after day. Caribou has legally hunted almost every major game animal in Africa except for rhino and toured on the USS Midway from 1978-1982, creating a ruckus all over the Pacific. He's toured Antarctica, stood his ground against grizzly bears... Even for an older fellow, he's packed quite a bit of excitement into his years on Earth. I'll probably get ahead of him tomorrow, but it has been very awesome hearing about his life over the past few nights.",
        "I don't believe I'll have many opportunities to post let alone get cell reception in the coming days but I'll see what I can do. I'll be done in three weeks so I'll try to squeeze one in!",
        "- Crush",
      ],
